@@ -0,0 +1,367 @@
+# The residue-A algebraization gate
+
+**Date:** 2026-08-23  
+**Characteristic target:** (0)  
+**Scope:** fixed (B=84), residue A, representative fiber `a00pp`, and the
+formal (q=2), (w=2) carrier direction.
+
+**Tier key.** **MOD-(p) EXACT** is a certified statement about the two banked
+finite fields only. **KNOWN THEOREM** is an implication whose hypotheses are
+stated. **OPEN** means that a required hypothesis or construction is not
+banked. **ROUTE OBSTRUCTED** means that a proposed method is known to be
+insufficient; it does not mean that the carrier itself is impossible.
+
+`sol-truth.md` predates the fully reconstructed D43 calculation. Its warning
+about the promotion chain remains correct, but its statement that the true D43
+family was unresolved is superseded by `sol-d43full.md`.
+
+## 0. Verdict
+
+
+\[
+ \boxed{
+ \begin{gathered}
+ X^{\rm full}_{43}(\mathbf F_{105337})\ne\varnothing,qquad
+ X^{\rm full}_{43}(\mathbf F_{105673})\ne\varnothing,\\
+ X^{\rm full}_{43}(\mathbf C)\ne\varnothing\quad\textbf{OPEN},\\
+ \text{compatible all-depth germ, algebraic branch, and polynomial Keller
+ pair}\quad\textbf{OPEN}.
+ \end{gathered}}
+\]
+
+There is no banked local obstruction to lifting the two witnesses, but there
+is also no smooth/horizontal-point certificate. The ranks (14) and (111)
+are **smooth-ish**, not a Jacobian-criterion proof.
+
+The cheapest decisive promotion is **(b), a certified (p)-adic/characteristic-
+zero lift of one (B=84) witness**. Concretely: construct the common integral
+reconstruction scheme, certify a standard-smooth neighborhood at one modular
+point, and invoke Hensel/formal smoothness. A (B=168) modular test would probe
+the scale axis while leaving the same arithmetic gate unresolved.
+
+The hardest stage is not local plane-branch realization. It is simultaneous
+**globalization plus polynomial Keller realization**: all local charts must be
+expansions of the same two polynomials and the global identity
+(J(f,g)\in\mathbf C^*) must hold. No existing Artin, Newton--Puiseux, or
+Abhyankar--Moh theorem performs that promotion.
+
+## 1. Q1: modular point to characteristic zero
+
+### 1.1 What the D43 certificate gives
+
+**MOD-(p) EXACT.** At each prime there is a (184)-coordinate point killing
+all (218=34+95+89) generators and all independent survivor/floor gates. The
+parked (28)-coordinate component has an explicit (mathbb A^{14})
+parametrization after choosing (W_1,W_2), and its Jacobian rank is (14).
+With the parked point fixed, the (184) graph rows have rank (111) in the
+(156) graph coordinates.
+
+The block form of the full Jacobian therefore gives only
+
+\[
+ \operatorname{rank}J_{\rm full}(\bar x)\ge 14+111=125,qquad
+ \dim T_{\bar x}X_{43}^{\rm full}\le184-125=59.              \tag{1.1}
+\]
+
+No equality is certified. In particular, the record does **not** give the
+local Krull dimension, the height of the localized ideal, or a local generating
+set of size equal to the Jacobian rank. The separate floor `window rank 125`
+is not identified in the bank as the full scheme Jacobian and cannot fill this
+gap.
+
+The completely linear (101)-variable slice basis also does not prove
+smoothness of the unsliced scheme: (43) nonpivot graph coordinates (and the
+coordinates absent from that prefix ring) were fixed for witness search. Its
+full unsliced replay proves membership, not transversality or local dimension.
+
+Thus the question “is the witness a smooth point of the characteristic-zero
+ideal?” is presently ill-posed: there is not yet a characteristic-zero point.
+The correct question is whether (ar x) is a point where an integral model is
+**smooth over the (p)-adic base**.
+
+### 1.2 The exact local lifting criterion
+
+First define a common finite-type model
+
+\[
+ X=\operatorname{Spec}R[z_1,\ldots,z_{184}]/I,qquad
+ R=\mathbf Z[1/N]\ \text{or a finite number-ring localization},       \tag{1.2}
+\]
+
+whose reductions give the two tested systems. The current artifacts are
+prime-specific reduced checkpoints and `.ms` files. Matching row counts,
+supports, and gates do not by themselves certify (1.2). The clean model should
+retain (W_i,uW_i) and their radical/inverse equations; arbitrary integer
+representatives of their modular values are not a characteristic-zero model.
+
+At a prime (mathfrak p\mid p), a cheapest positive certificate is a
+**standard-smooth presentation near (ar x)**:
+
+1. compute the full relative Jacobian rank (c) at (ar x);
+2. find (c) local generators (F_{i_1},\ldots,F_{i_c}) and (c) variables
+   with a (c\times c) Jacobian minor nonzero modulo (mathfrak p);
+3. certify after localizing at that minor that all (218) generators generate
+   the same ideal as those (c) equations (equivalently certify local
+   dimension (184-c), local complete-intersection generation, and
+   (mathfrak p)-flatness).
+
+Then (X\to\operatorname{Spec}R) is smooth at (ar x). Smoothness is
+equivalent, for a finite-presentation morphism, to formal smoothness; hence
+(ar x) lifts compatibly through every (R/\mathfrak p^n), giving an
+(R_{\mathfrak p}^{\wedge})-point and therefore a characteristic-zero point.
+This is the applicable Hensel/implicit-function theorem, not merely a high
+Jacobian subrank. See the standard-smooth Jacobian presentation and the
+[infinitesimal lifting criterion](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02H6).
+
+Two cautions are load-bearing.
+
+- Positive dimension is **not** a lifting obstruction. A smooth
+  positive-dimensional point is the favorable case: its free coordinates can
+  be lifted arbitrarily and the pivot coordinates follow.
+- Singularity is not itself a proof of nonliftability. It only removes the
+  automatic Hensel implication. Conversely, a point can be smooth on the
+  special fiber and still lie on a vertical component; flatness/relative
+  smoothness is what excludes this.
+
+### 1.3 The cheapest obstruction screen
+
+After (1.2) is available, choose integral lifts (x_1) of (ar x). The first
+correction equation for a lift modulo (p^2) is
+
+\[
+ J(\bar x)\,\delta
+ =-\frac{F(x_1)}p\pmod p.                              \tag{1.3}
+\]
+
+Failure of (1.3), with all (218) rows and all integral syzygies respected, is
+a genuine first-order obstruction to lifting **that point**. It does not prove
+that the whole characteristic-zero family is empty. Success is a useful
+screen, but without formal smoothness it proves only a lift to (p^2); a later
+obstruction may occur.
+
+Accordingly:
+
+| certificate | what it proves | status |
+|---|---|---|
+| solve (1.3) | this witness reaches (p^2) | **CHEAP SCREEN / NOT DECISIVE POSITIVELY** |
+| standard-smooth certificate at one prime | compatible lifts to all (p^n), hence (X_{43}(\mathbf C)\ne\varnothing) | **CHEAPEST DECISIVE POSITIVE TEST** |
+| explicit exact (\overline{\mathbf Q})-point and replay | (X_{43}(\mathbf C)\ne\varnothing) | **DECISIVE**, probably dearer |
+| (1\in I\otimes\mathbf Q) with a rational Nullstellensatz certificate | (X_{43}(\mathbf C)=\varnothing) | **DECISIVE NEGATIVE FOR THE WHOLE FIXED-D43 SCHEME** |
+
+### 1.4 Why two primes and CRT do not promote the result
+
+If (I\otimes\mathbf Q=(1)), clearing denominators gives some nonzero
+integer (M\in I). A reduction can be nonempty only at primes dividing an
+appropriate such bad integer. Nonemptiness at two large primes is therefore
+good robustness evidence, but without an effective bound excluding both
+primes as bad it is not a proof of horizontal nonemptiness.
+
+CRT combines residues; it does not identify points on the two fibers. Rational
+reconstruction would require all of the following, none banked:
+
+- a common integral ideal and matched component;
+- a priori height/denominator bounds;
+- a reason the desired point is rational rather than algebraic or (p)-adic.
+
+An actual (mathbf Z_p)-point is enough: it makes (I\otimes\mathbf Q)
+proper, hence the finite-type (mathbf Q)-scheme and its base change to
+(mathbf C) are nonempty. Finite CRT data alone do not.
+
+## 2. Q2: characteristic-zero jet to polynomial Keller pair
+
+The lifting chain has distinct gates. None may be merged with the next.
+
+| stage | required object/certificate | status |
+|---:|---|---|
+| 0 | Full D43 witness at (p=105337,105673) | **MOD-(p) EXACT / DONE** |
+| 1 | Common integral/number-ring reconstruction scheme whose reductions are the tested systems | **OPEN / CERTIFICATE ABSENT** |
+| 2 | One characteristic-zero point of the finite D43 scheme | **OPEN**; relative smoothness would settle it by a **KNOWN THEOREM** |
+| 3 | Compatible points at every depth (D\), not merely a nonempty (X_D) chosen independently | **OPEN** |
+| 4 | Inverse-limit coefficients satisfying every reconstruction, no-log, primitivity, and survivor equation: a genuine formal Puiseux germ | **OPEN**; follows formally from a compatible inverse limit only after reconstruction fidelity is proved |
+| 5 | The prescribed formal series are convergent and algebraic over the appropriate rational-function field | **OPEN FOR THIS CARRIER** |
+| 6 | All pole charts, the shared trunk/merge, conjugates, intersection contacts, and the (w=2) quotient glue on one global compactification, with no missing/extra places | **OPEN** |
+| 7 | The global data come from the same (f,g\in\mathbf C[x,y]), with the required Sigray rectangles, and every coefficient of (J(f,g)-j) vanishes for one (j\ne0) | **OPEN; HARDEST** |
+| 8 | Such pairs exist along unbounded carrier scale, e.g. (kappa_i(r)=42\,2^r) with (B_r\to\infty) | **OPEN**; required to refute A-SCALE |
+
+### 2.1 Inverse-limit survival is a new equation problem
+
+D43 is one finite jet. It does not assert that (X_{45},X_{47},\ldots) are
+nonempty, much less that the truncation maps contain a compatible chain
+
+\[
+ \cdots\longrightarrow x_{47}\longrightarrow x_{45}
+ \longrightarrow x_{43}.                               \tag{2.1}
+\]
+
+Nonemptiness at every depth would imply formal consistency only after the
+systems are proved nested and one controls coefficient fields/compatibility;
+the direct certificate is an actual point of the inverse limit. Formal
+smoothness of every transition would be a known sufficient mechanism. The
+banked (ell^+\ge37) says D75 is the first possible depth for the named Newton
+certification; it is not an extension theorem and not a kill.
+
+### 2.2 Algebraicity is stronger than finite-jet realizability
+
+Any one finite Puiseux jet with finite denominator can be completed to a
+finite (t)-parametrization and eliminated to produce **some** algebraic plane
+branch with that jet. Likewise, admissible finite plane-branch topological
+types are locally realizable. This cheap local fact does not preserve the
+all-depth reconstructed coefficients, the second pole, or the Keller identity.
+
+For an all-depth series, three notions differ:
+
+\[
+ \text{formal}\quad\not\Rightarrow\quad
+ \text{convergent}\quad\not\Rightarrow\quad\text{algebraic}.           \tag{2.2}
+\]
+
+For a complex algebraic plane branch, Newton--Puiseux gives a convergent
+Puiseux expansion. It does not say that an arbitrary formal expansion is
+algebraic. Thus algebraicity would dispose of convergence here, but neither is
+banked.
+
+Artin approximation is a **KNOWN THEOREM but not the missing promotion**. For
+a formal solution of a suitable finite analytic/algebraic system, it produces,
+for each prescribed finite order, a convergent/algebraic solution agreeing to
+that order. It need not make the given formal series convergent, need not give
+one solution agreeing at all orders, and does not impose global polynomiality.
+This finite-order limitation is exactly why D43 plus Artin does not produce a
+Keller pair.
+
+### 2.3 The passport is already algebraic; the carrier is not
+
+The residual quotient
+
+\[
+ \beta(u)=\frac{u(u-2/3)^3}{(u^2-u+1/6)^2}
+\]
+
+is an explicit rational map with passport
+({(3,1),(2,2),(3,1)}). This stage is **EXACT / DONE**. It supplies no
+algebraization of the common carrier because
+
+\[
+ \frac{(C^4h_1)^3}{(C^3f)^4}=\frac{h_1^3}{f^4}.             \tag{2.3}
+\]
+
+The quotient literally forgets the characteristic factors of (C); the
+banked factor (7) is already absent from the passport. Belyi/Riemann
+existence therefore solves the wrong realization problem.
+
+### 2.4 Global compatibility and polynomiality
+
+Even algebraic local branches must be shown to arise simultaneously from one
+global object. Required compatibility includes at least:
+
+- one common normalization and one common infinity divisor;
+- the two poles, shared characteristic trunk, merge, conjugates, and contacts;
+- the genus, conductor, intersection, and pole-divisor ledgers at every chart;
+- absence of extra finite asymptotic punctures or unrecorded infinity branches;
+- descent of all local coefficient choices to the same global (f) and (g).
+
+After gluing, polynomiality still requires the resulting functions to be
+regular on the affine plane, to have only the prescribed poles at infinity,
+and to have finite support in the Sigray rectangles. Finally the global
+polynomial identity (J(f,g)=j) must be replayed, not inferred from a finite
+set of branchwise valuations.
+
+No Newton-polygon realization theorem gives this simultaneous two-function
+Keller realization. Abhyankar--Moh concerns an embedded affine line in the
+plane; the residue-A generic fiber is a multi-place configuration, and the
+theorem neither glues the two-pole tree nor constructs a second polynomial
+with constant Jacobian. Its use here is **ROUTE OBSTRUCTED / WRONG
+HYPOTHESES**, not an obstruction to the carrier.
+
+This is the hardest gate. A positive (B=84) realization in the intended
+nonproper residue-A class would already be an actual plane-Jacobian-
+Conjecture counterexample; there is no routine theorem expected to manufacture
+it from local data.
+
+## 3. Q3: obstruction, verdict, and next move
+
+### 3.1 Candidate obstructions
+
+No obstruction is presently detected by the D43 witnesses. The available
+candidate *theorems* are:
+
+| candidate | theorem needed | present disposition |
+|---|---|---|
+| vertical/singular modular component | failure of the all-row correction (1.3), or proof that every special-fiber component is vertical | **UNTESTED**; would kill only the affected fixed-depth component unless family-wide |
+| eventual depth death | a finite (D) with a certified unit ideal, or failure of every compatible inverse-limit branch | **OPEN**; D43 survives and (ell^+\ge37) is not such a theorem |
+| nonalgebraic formal coefficients | a finite-determinacy, recurrence, or differential-algebraic theorem excluding every algebraic Puiseux completion with this reconstruction | **NO SUCH THEOREM BANKED** |
+| **GCT-A** | every polynomial-origin excess carrier globalizes to a Hénon coordinate tail; degree minimality then removes it and gives (K_A=42) | **CONJECTURAL** |
+| **K2C / A-SCALE** | polynomial origin plus the pure boundary Jacobian identity uniformly bounds the number/product of internal characteristic indices, or bounds (B) | **CONJECTURAL** |
+| **A-CONDUCTOR** | a scale-independent upper bound on the residue-A pole conductors | **CONJECTURAL**; the proved inequality (c(P_i)\ge2(\kappa_i-1)) has the wrong sign |
+
+GCT-A is the sharpest named algebraization obstruction: combined with
+degree-minimality it forbids every excess (q=2) insertion. But
+`sol-ucda.md` also proves why this is genuinely new. Type ((2,3)) cusp
+protection makes every rectangular realization orbitwise degree-minimal at
+every scale; a local approximate root is not a global coordinate tail.
+Neither Abhyankar--Moh nor the leading-form automorphism calculus supplies
+GCT-A.
+
+The other standard ledgers do not currently favor nonalgebraization:
+
+- (\operatorname{ord}_t f_y=3-\kappa_i) remains compatible as scale grows;
+- the boundary cancellation order (5B-2) grows with (B);
+- the genus/conductor budget grows with the polynomial degree;
+- the fixed Belyi quotient cancels the carrier exactly.
+
+These facts remove proposed easy obstructions. They are not positive evidence
+that a polynomial Keller pair exists.
+
+### 3.2 Honest verdict
+
+**VERDICT.** The correct state is
+
+\[
+ \boxed{\textbf{no known obstruction and no known construction}.}
+\]
+
+At the finite-jet, fixed-(B) level the carrier is robust: it survives the
+fully reconstructed D43 system at two primes and passes independent replay.
+The two similar rank profiles mildly disfavor a one-prime accident. They say
+nothing decisive about horizontal lifting, all-depth survival, algebraicity,
+or global polynomial origin.
+
+Therefore the carrier currently looks **modularly viable**, not
+**algebraizable**. There is no calibrated basis for either optimism or
+pessimism at the polynomial Keller tier.
+
+### 3.3 Single next step
+
+Choose **(b): characteristic-zero lift of the (B=84) witness**, in this exact
+order:
+
+1. re-emit the (218) equations over a common (R) or directly over
+   (mathbf Z_p), retaining the radical variables and audit their reductions;
+2. run the all-row (p^2) correction test (1.3);
+3. compute the full Jacobian rank and local dimension at the witness;
+4. emit a nonzero minor plus localized-generation/flatness certificate.
+
+If step 4 succeeds at either prime, Hensel gives the first certified
+characteristic-zero D43 point. If step 2 fails, it exposes an immediate local
+obstruction worth deciding family-wide. If the point is singular but reaches
+(p^2), search the same modular component for a relatively smooth point before
+attempting deep singular deformation theory.
+
+Option (a), the (B=168,\ \kappa=84) modular test, is the next **scale** test,
+not the next **algebraization** test: either verdict would remain modular.
+Option (c) would be ultimately decisive, but GCT-A/K2C/A-CONDUCTOR presently
+name research programs rather than a bounded certificate computation.
+
+The promotion ledger is consequently
+
+\[
+ \boxed{
+ \text{two-prime D43 point}
+ \;\xrightarrow[\textbf{next}]{\text{relative smoothness / Hensel}}\;
+ \text{char-0 D43 point}
+ \;\xrightarrow[\textbf{open}]{\varprojlim D}\;
+ \text{formal germ}
+ \;\xrightarrow[\textbf{open}]{\text{algebraize + glue}}\;
+ \text{global algebraic data}
+ \;\xrightarrow[\textbf{hardest/open}]{\text{polynomial Keller}}\;
+ (f,g).}
+\]

